---
source: crates/rumps-query/tests/scripts.rs
description: "; Partial application as a higher-order function callback\n; A partially applied function is a first-class value and can be passed\n; directly to HoFs like `Mappable:map` and `Filterable:filter`.\n\nfun add (a: Int, b: Int) -> Int { a + b }\nfun gt (a: Int, b: Int) -> Bool { b > a }\n\nfun main() {\n  ; `add(5)` is `(Int) -> Int`; pass directly to `Mappable:map`\n  write Mappable:map(add(5), [1, 2, 3])\n  ; Expected: [6, 7, 8]\n\n  ; `gt(2)` is `(Int) -> Bool`; pass directly to `Filterable:filter`\n  write Filterable:filter(gt(2), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n  ; Expected: [3, 4, 5]\n\n  ; Binary named function passed directly to `reduce`; no partial app\n  ; needed, but this verifies first-class use still works\n  write Foldable:fold(add, 0, [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n  ; Expected: 15\n\n  ; Stored partial passed through pipe into a HoF\n  let add10 = add(10)\n  write [1, 2, 3] |> Mappable:map(add10)\n  ; Expected: [11, 12, 13]\n\n  ; Partial passed to `reduce`; `add` is already binary, so no partial,\n  ; but `add3` partial works as binary reducer\n  write Foldable:fold(add, 100, [1, 2, 3])\n  ; Expected: 106\n}\n"
---
[ 6, 7, 8 ]
[ 3, 4, 5 ]
15
[ 11, 12, 13 ]
106
